  "accepted_answer": {
    "post_number": 2,
    "username": "AtulPS",
    "name": null,
    "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/discourse.onlinedegree.iitm.ac.in/atulps/{size}/66642_2.png",
    "excerpt": "<p>Let’s break it down step by step:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><code>echo 'Hello, World'</code></strong>:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>This part simply prints the string <code>'Hello, World'</code> to the standard output (stdout).</li>\n</ul>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong><code>&gt; out</code></strong>:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><code>&gt;</code> is the redirection operator for output. It takes the output from <code>echo</code> (normally stdout) and redirects it to a file named <code>out</code>. If the file doesn’t exist, it creates it. If the file already exists, it overwrites its content.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong><code>1&gt;&amp;2</code></strong>:</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>This part redirects file descriptor <code>1</code> (stdout) to file descriptor <code>2</code> (stderr).</li>\n<li><code>1</code> represents stdout and <code>2</code> represents stderr.</li>\n<li><code>&gt;&amp;</code> is used to redirect one file descriptor to another. So, <code>1&gt;&amp;2</code> says, “redirect stdout to stderr.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So finally Hello, World will print in the terminal as standard error and out file will remain empty.</p>",
    "accepter_name": null,
    "accepter_username": "21f2001068"
  },
